Dataset ecommerce {
  label: 'ecommerce (docs RLP example)'
  description: '''
    The Holistics docs example:
    https://docs.holistics.io/docs/joins/filter-direction#the-problem-rlp-cannot-reach-dimensions-behind-a-one-way-relationship

    Structure copied exactly: the same six one_way relationships, the same total_orders metric, the same
    permission on dim_cities.region matching the `region` user attribute.

    Only two model names differ from the doc, because the doc's names already exist elsewhere in this
    project (demo (specific use-case)/prusa/models/) and the duplicates raise an error:
      fact_orders  -> fact_orders_test
      dim_products -> dim_products_test

    THE TEST, in production as an Explorer/Viewer. Set the test user's `region` attribute to 'North' first,
    or the baseline returns 0 instead of 2.

      1. Total Orders, no breakdown.
           2  -> RLP enforced (North only). Baseline confirmed.
           4  -> RLP not enforced; nothing below is meaningful.
           0  -> the user's region attribute value does not match 'North'.

      2. Total Orders broken down by Dim Products Test -> Name, with NO city field. The doc's failing query:

           explore {
             dimensions { dim_products_test.name }
             measures   { total_orders }
           }

         Docs say this must fail with "Some permission rules are not applied in this Explore. This is likely
         due to a missing relationship between the models."

         EXPECTED TO PASS, NOT FAIL. Per Khai (2026-09-04) the docs are wrong here and he is updating them:
         omitting rlp_propagation means 'two_way', not inherit-from-filter_direction, so dim_products_test
         IS reachable and no error should be raised. This dataset is now a control that DEMONSTRATES the
         doc error rather than confirming the doc.

           No error -> confirms Khai's correction. The doc's example, copied exactly, does not behave as
                       the doc claims.
           Errors   -> then the correction does not hold on this workspace and the behaviour is
                       version-dependent. Report that.

         To see the documented error, add rlp_propagation='one_way' to
         relationship(fact_orders_test.product_id > dim_products_test.id, ...) and re-run.

      3. Total Qty from Fact Inventory - that fact has no path to dim_cities at all, the doc graph's
         equivalent of the customer's benchmark facts. Expect 110 (50 + 60), and note whether it errors.
  '''
  data_source_name: 'demodb'

  models: [
    dim_users, dim_products_test, dim_cities,
    dim_merchants, dim_categories,
    fact_orders_test, fact_inventory
  ]

  relationships: [
    relationship(fact_orders_test.user_id > dim_users.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_orders_test.product_id > dim_products_test.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_inventory.product_id > dim_products_test.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(dim_users.city_id > dim_cities.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(dim_products_test.merchant_id > dim_merchants.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(dim_products_test.category_id > dim_categories.id, true, 'one_way')
  ]

  metric total_orders {
    label: 'Total Orders'
    type: 'number'
    definition: @aql count(fact_orders_test.id);;
  }

  permission regional_access {
    field: r(dim_cities.region)
    operator: 'matches_user_attribute'
    value: 'region' // user attribute
  }
}
